.TH _PRINTF 3 "September 2026" "Holberton School" "_printf Manual"
.SH NAME
_printf \- formatted output conversion

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"

.B int _printf(const char *format, ...);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf
function produces output according to a format string and writes the output
to the standard output stream.

The format string is composed of zero or more directives. Ordinary characters
(not preceded by %) are copied to the output. Conversion specifiers begin with
a percent character (%) and are replaced with formatted output.

This implementation of
.B _printf
supports a limited set of conversion specifiers.

.SH CONVERSION SPECIFIERS
The following conversion specifiers are supported:

.TP
.B c
Prints a single character.

.TP
.B s
Prints a string. If the string argument is NULL, "(null)" is printed.

.TP
.B %
Prints a literal percent sign.

.TP
.B d, i
Prints a signed decimal integer.

.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful return,
.B _printf
returns the number of characters printed, excluding the null byte used to end
output to strings.

If
.I format
is NULL, the function returns -1.

If a percent character is followed by an unknown specifier, the percent sign
and the unknown character are printed.

If a percent character is the last character of the format string, no output
is produced and -1 is returned.

.SH ERRORS
No explicit error handling is performed other than returning -1 when the format
string is NULL or ends with an incomplete conversion specifier.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates basic usage of
.B _printf :

.nf
_printf("Hello, %s!\n", "world");
_printf("Value: %d\n", 42);
_printf("Percent: %%\n");
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO
printf(3)

.SH AUTHORS
Written by Holberton School students as part of the printf project.

.SH BUGS
This implementation does not support flags, field width, precision, or length
modifiers.

